			<div class="content" id="post-content-3650389">
									

<p>I have mixed feelings on this one.</p>
<p>On the upside, the descriptions of the entities and the implications of the corporal's actions were neat, has good build-up, and were very unsettling. So that's solid.</p>
<p>On the downside, pretty much everything for the first half could've used more work. Your grammar is loose and your style/tone is more casual than official. For example, you just keep stating that we should remember bureaucratic niceties, but not in a way that feels worked in or procedural. Worse, it feels like you haven't done any work at all creating plausibility with the military/technical stuff.</p>
<p>I'm not saying I need ten paragraphs of milspeak for this to work, the <em>amount</em> of detail you've provided is totally fine. I'm also absolutely not a military expert and I'm not asking for &quot;realism,&quot; but there were tons of little problems that felt lazy and kept me from getting into it.</p>
<p>No vote.</p>
<p>(Also, when you have the translator muttering &quot;Shai a teen&quot;, 1: the scared superstitious translator is a pretty lame trope, 2: real generic thing to say, and 3: not at lest footnoting that feels like you're trying to give us readers a nod, but mostly just making the UIU look like morons. Although if you did footnote it, see 2.)</p>

							</div>

								
