<p>Sure, a few examples:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>At time of writing, this event is still classified as a standard Extranormal Event, requiring no special containment procedures, however, should this anomaly reappear, reclassification to SCP status may take place.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All of this is basically self evident, given that this is EE. If you really want to include this, you could make up formatting. E.g., &quot;Containment Directives: None (containment procedures are pending SCP classification)&quot; or something like that. That's not a super great example, I'm just saying, using bureaucratic structure and jargon helps build tone and can get the same work done with less text.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Because this interview was conducted by the United States' Unusual Incidents Unit, it should be noted that standard Foundation guidelines were not followed, and the interviewee has chosen to remain anonymous, referred to in the document as 'Charlie'2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Plus</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the individual I am interviewing has chosen to remain anonymous, but will be referred to as 'Charlie' for the duration of this interview. Do you agree to these terms?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You've said this whole thing twice now, plus the subject: Charlie in between. Just think of which was the more interesting way to say that, and cut or partially footnote the other.</p>
<p>As for the scared superstitious local guide/translator thing, it might not be as common on the site, but it definitely goes back as least as far as the pulp days (1910's/20's) and probably into the 1800's. It's not the worst thing on earth to use well-worn horror tropes for world-building, but some nuance would help.</p>

							</div>

								
