<p>You have my +1</p>
<p>As far as military stuff, the biggest thing that stands out to me the radio chatter. Generally speaking, you'll never state your name over the radio. Even when comm is set up well, radio tends to sound pretty garbled. For this reason, radio chatter is often very concise and, since it's a pain articulate full sentences in such a way that the distant end can understand.</p>
<p>A good rule is &quot;you, you, this is me&quot; to start the broadcast. In this instance, Cpl Warren can be referred to as Echo 4 Whiskey (denoting rank, and initial of last name) and Lt Chambers as Oscar 2 Charlie. So that first broadcast might go something like &quot;Echo 4 Whiskey, Echo 4 Whiskey, this is is Oscar 2 Charlie. Radio check. Over&quot;</p>
<p>I could go on longer, but it'd probably cram up the discussion. PM me if you'd like more feedback in regards to military stuff.</p>

